WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.240
Adult rodents make new neurons.

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.660
Do adult humans make them too?

3
00:00:08.683 --> 00:00:12.383
BrdU cannot be given to healthy people for research.

4
00:00:13.417 --> 00:00:17.917
But in the 1990s, some cancer patients received it for clinical reasons.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.400
After death, their dentate gyrus held cells with both BrdU and a neuron protein.

6
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.200
Both facts, in single human cells.

7
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:34.020
Bomb tests in the 1950s and early 1960s sharply raised the carbon-14 in the air.

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.080
That carbon ends up in the DNA of dividing cells.

9
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.760
A neuron's DNA keeps the level of its last division.

10
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:46.733
Neurons at the back of the cortex match the person's own birth year.

11
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:48.633
They were born around then.

12
00:00:49.373 --> 00:00:50.933
So were olfactory bulb neurons.

13
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:53.243
Pause and predict.

14
00:00:54.263 --> 00:00:56.803
Hippocampal neurons do not match the birth year.

15
00:00:57.643 --> 00:00:59.203
What can you conclude, and what not?

16
00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:10.473
An offset beyond measurement error points to neurons added after birth, unless

17
00:01:10.473 --> 00:01:12.793
repair or stray nuclei explain it.

18
00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:17.573
But a sample averages many nuclei, so it cannot say how many, or when.

19
00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:26.767
One model estimate is about 700 new neurons per hippocampus per day.

20
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.880
Stains for young neurons show a state, not a birth date.

21
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.680
In people, it is debated whether they mark only new neurons.

22
00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:37.153
And the stains disagree.

23
00:01:38.113 --> 00:01:39.613
One study found none in adults.

24
00:01:40.553 --> 00:01:41.553
Others found thousands.

25
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:43.753
Pause and predict.

26
00:01:44.953 --> 00:01:49.553
Suppose 700 new neurons arrive each day, and each stays young and marked for weeks

27
00:01:49.553 --> 00:01:53.333
or months. How many marked cells should one hippocampus hold?

28
00:01:59.717 --> 00:02:05.277
Rate times time. Even three weeks gives about 15,000 across one hippocampus.

29
00:02:06.497 --> 00:02:07.497
These are toy numbers.

30
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.580
So a count of none needs an explanation.

31
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.040
A far lower rate in those donors.

32
00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:18.053
A young-neuron protein lost when tissue waited, or was fixed for long.

33
00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:22.027
Or a young state that is short, or unmarked, in people.

34
00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:26.090
Or sections and stains that missed cells that were there.

35
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.550
The human evidence that carries a birth record points to some new neurons.

36
00:02:35.633 --> 00:02:39.293
How many new neurons people make, and what they do, is still open.

37
00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:45.097
Takeaway: ask whether the same cells carry a birth record and a neuron identity.
